/* "Go deeper" material, part 3: SEO Fundamentals, link building, content, analytics, AI search. */
(function () {
  var QF = window.QF; QF.deep = QF.deep || {};
  var D = QF.deep;

  /* ---------- SEO Fundamentals ---------- */
  D['seo-fundamentals|0|0'] = [
    'Crawlers decide **what to fetch and how often** using signals such as how important a URL looks (links pointing to it), how often it changes, how fast and healthy your server is, and how many URLs they have waiting. A page linked from your homepage and refreshed daily is revisited far more often than a page buried six clicks deep that has not changed in two years. This is why internal linking and fresh, useful updates are SEO levers and not just usability.',
    'Crawlers follow links in the HTML (`<a href>`), not buttons that run JavaScript to navigate, and they respect `robots.txt`, which tells them what not to fetch. They also use sitemaps as a list of suggestions. A server that is slow or returns many errors makes Google crawl more cautiously, so reliability is part of crawlability.',
    'In practice, diagnose crawling by looking at three things: whether important URLs are reachable by links, whether anything blocks them (robots.txt, login walls, errors), and whether Search Console shows them as discovered or crawled. If a page has never been crawled, the problem is nearly always discovery (no links) or access (blocked), not quality.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|0|1'] = [
    'Indexing is not a yes or no switch for the whole site; it happens **per URL**, and a page can be crawled and still be left out. Common reasons are `noindex`, duplicates (Google chooses one canonical version and drops the rest), thin or low-value content, soft 404s and pages Google decides are not worth storing. The Pages report in Search Console states the reason, and "Crawled - currently not indexed" is usually a quality or value judgment.',
    'During processing Google works out what a page is about, which language it is in, what its main content is, and which other pages are similar. It may **canonicalize** several URLs into one and show that one in results. This is why consistent internal links, redirects and canonical tags matter: they help Google choose the version you intend.',
    'Do not panic about every excluded URL. Many exclusions are healthy (parameter URLs, redirects, pages you set to noindex on purpose). Focus on whether the pages that **should** earn traffic are indexed. Check them with URL Inspection, and if they are not, fix the specific cause rather than repeatedly requesting indexing.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|0|2'] = [
    'Ranking is a process of **retrieval, scoring and re-ranking** performed at the moment of the search. The system first finds candidate pages that could match the query, then scores them with many signals, then adjusts the list for things like diversity, freshness, location and the type of results wanted. That is why the same page can rank first for one wording and not appear for another.',
    'Google describes using many systems rather than a single formula: systems that understand language and meaning, link analysis, systems that favor helpful, reliable content, and systems that demote spam. No outsider knows the weights, so treat claims of "the #1 ranking factor" with caution and prefer official documentation, controlled tests and your own data.',
    'The practical conclusion is to improve the **whole page experience for the searcher**: match intent, be clearly better than the current results, make the page easy to use, and earn trust. When rankings change, ask what changed on the SERP (new features, new competitors, a new meaning for the query) before assuming something is wrong with your page.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|0|3'] = [
    'Build a **diagnosis tree** and use it every time. Not crawled: check links, robots.txt, server access and whether the URL is in your sitemap. Crawled but not indexed: check noindex, canonical (is another page chosen?), duplicate content, thin content and soft 404. Indexed but not ranking for the target: check search intent match, content quality against the current results, internal links, and the competitiveness of the query.',
    'Use the right evidence at each step. URL Inspection shows Google\'s last crawl, the indexed status and the selected canonical, and the **live test** shows what Google can fetch now. A `site:` search is only a rough hint. Server logs show whether Googlebot has actually requested the page.',
    'Avoid common wrong turns: requesting indexing again and again, adding more keywords to a page that does not match intent, and building links to a page that is blocked or noindexed. Fix the first broken stage first. A note in your project log (date, symptom, cause, fix) turns each case into a pattern you will recognize next time.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|1|0'] = [
    'The results page is a **layout the search engine builds for each query**, and the layout tells you what it believes people want. A grid of products and shopping ads means purchase intent; a map and three listings means local; video thumbnails mean people want to watch; an AI summary at the top means the system thinks a direct answer is useful. Each feature takes attention from the classic blue links.',
    'This changes how you set goals. For a query with a strong answer box, the **click opportunity** may be small even at position one, and visibility inside the feature (a featured snippet, a video, a local pack) may matter more than the blue link. For queries dominated by ads and shopping, organic clicks sit far down the page. Estimate realistic traffic by looking at the actual layout, not just the position.',
    'Record the layout in your research notes (screenshot with the date, location and device). Layouts change often, differ by device and location, and are personalized to a degree. When a client says "we dropped", compare the current SERP with an earlier screenshot, because sometimes the ranking is the same and the page simply got more crowded.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|1|1'] = [
    'Think of ranking signals in layers. **Relevance** signals ask whether the page and its words match the query and its likely goal. **Quality and trust** signals ask whether it is original, accurate, expertly made and reputable. **Authority** signals include links and mentions from other sites. **Experience** signals include speed, mobile usability, security and a stable layout. **Context** signals include location, language, device and time.',
    'These layers interact. A relevant page on a site with poor trust may not rank; an authoritative site with an off-topic page will not rank either. That is why a single tactic (more keywords, more links, faster pages) rarely moves a result on its own and why audits look at all layers.',
    'Be careful with lists of "200 ranking factors". Many are guesses, correlations or outdated. Use them as prompts for what to check, not as a recipe. The most reliable guide is the intent and quality of the pages that already rank: if they all do something your page does not (a comparison table, original photos, recent data), that is evidence worth acting on.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|1|2'] = [
    'Myths survive because search is opaque and confident voices are rewarded. A useful habit is to **grade every claim** by source quality: official documentation first, then controlled experiments with a clear method, then case studies with context, then anecdotes and opinions. Ask what would prove the claim wrong and whether anyone has tested it.',
    'Examples worth knowing: the meta keywords tag is ignored by Google; there is no official "duplicate content penalty" for ordinary duplication (Google chooses one version, while deliberately manipulative duplication can be spam); the length of a page is not a ranking factor by itself; and social media shares are not a direct ranking signal. Each has nuance, but the headline versions are widely repeated wrongly.',
    'Test locally when stakes are low. Pick similar pages, change one thing on half of them, wait, and compare. Even small tests teach you how your own site behaves. Keep a list of claims you have checked and what you found, because clients will quote myths to you and a calm, evidence-based answer builds trust.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|2|0'] = [
    'The four pillars (technical, on-page, content, off-page) are a way to organize work, but real projects need **sequencing**. A sensible order is: first make sure the site can be crawled, indexed and used on a phone (technical), then make sure each key page targets a clear intent and is well structured (on-page), then fill gaps with new, helpful content, and build reputation through links and mentions as the content earns them.',
    'Resources decide the mix. A small business with a strong site and no content should write; a large store with thousands of duplicate URLs should fix architecture first; a new site with no authority needs a long-term plan for earned links and brand. An audit that scores each pillar from one to five on the main pages gives an honest starting picture.',
    'Make the pillars visible in reporting. List completed and planned tasks under each pillar with an expected impact, so a client can see why you are spending a month on redirects and not on new blog posts. When one pillar is clearly the bottleneck, say so plainly and put the effort there.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|2|1'] = [
    'Write goals as **outcomes with numbers, a baseline and a date**: "Increase organic leads from 18 to 30 a month by June" rather than "improve rankings". Break them into **leading indicators** you can influence within weeks (indexed pages, impressions, rankings, click-through rate) and **lagging indicators** that prove business value (leads, sales, revenue). Leading indicators keep a project honest in the early months when lagging ones have not moved yet.',
    'Capture the baseline from multiple sources: Search Console for clicks, impressions and queries (export 16 months while you can), analytics for organic sessions and conversions, a rank tracker for priority terms, a crawl for site health, and the CRM or order system for real leads and sales. Save dated exports and screenshots so you can show where you started even if tools change.',
    'Choose a small number of KPIs (three to five). A dashboard with 40 metrics gives no direction. Agree how each will be measured and who is responsible for the data. Reviewing KPIs monthly and the goal quarterly keeps you honest about whether the strategy still fits the business.'
  ];
  D['seo-fundamentals|2|2'] = [
    'A tidy setup in week one saves months later. In **Search Console** verify the domain property with DNS, submit the sitemap and check the Pages report. In **GA4** set the data retention to 14 months, mark real conversions as key events, link Search Console and exclude internal traffic. Add **Bing Webmaster Tools** by importing from Search Console. Install a free crawler and run a first crawl. Save all baseline exports.',
    'For the first 30 days, a realistic plan is: week one setup and baseline; week two fix critical technical problems (indexing blockers, redirects, errors); week three keyword research and a keyword map; week four optimize titles, headings and internal links on the top pages, and plan the next quarter\'s content. Resist the temptation to start link building before the site is sound.',
    'Keep a **project log** from day one with dates, changes, reasons and results. It becomes the evidence that connects effort to outcomes, and it helps you answer "what did you do in March?" in seconds. Add a short checklist of recurring tasks (monthly report, search term review, crawl, backup check) so routine work does not depend on memory.'
  ];

  /* ---------- Link building ---------- */
  D['link-building|0|0'] = [
    'Links remain a core signal because they are hard to fake at scale: another site had to decide your page was worth mentioning. Google\'s early PageRank idea (a page is important if important pages link to it) still shapes link analysis, although modern systems also weigh relevance, context and the trustworthiness of the linking site, and Google says it neutralizes a lot of spam automatically.',
    'Attributes are hints. `rel="nofollow"` says you do not endorse the target, `rel="sponsored"` marks paid or affiliate links, and `rel="ugc"` marks user-generated content. Google treats these as hints, so a nofollow link may still help discovery or have some value, but you should not count on it. Do not use these attributes to hide paid links without disclosure; mark paid placements correctly.',
    'Anchor text gives context, but a natural profile is mostly **branded**, **URL-based** and **descriptive or generic** anchors, with exact-match keyword anchors a small minority. A profile with hundreds of identical commercial anchors from unrelated sites looks manufactured. Quality, relevance and variety matter more than volume, and the best links are usually earned by something worth citing.'
  ];
  D['link-building|0|1'] = [
    'Judge a prospective link on **five questions**: Is the site relevant to my topic? Does it have a real audience and real organic traffic? Is the link editorial (placed by someone choosing it) rather than bought? Is the page it would appear on genuine and not a thin page created for links? Is it a site I would be happy to be seen alongside? A "yes" to most is a strong sign.',
    'Third-party authority scores (DR, DA, Authority Score) are **vendor estimates**, useful for comparing prospects quickly but unknown to Google. A site with a high score and collapsing organic traffic can be a poor choice, while a small, relevant local site with real readers may be excellent. Look at the site\'s own search visibility trend and the quality of its outbound links.',
    'Red flags include sites that openly sell guest posts on any topic, "write for us" pages with pricing, link networks with the same footer or design, pages with dozens of unrelated outbound links, and a sudden spike of links with identical anchors. When unsure, skip it. A handful of strong links beats a hundred weak ones, and weak ones can waste effort or create risk.'
  ];
  D['link-building|0|2'] = [
    '**Link intersect** analysis lists domains that link to two or three competitors but not to you. These are proven prospects because they clearly care about your topic and have linked to similar businesses. Combine it with a look at **what each competitor page was linked for**: a statistic, a tool, a guide, a resource list, a news mention. Then ask whether you can offer something equal or better.',
    'Sort the prospects by tactic: editorial mentions (need something worth citing), resource pages (need to fit and ask politely), directories and associations (membership), partnerships and suppliers (relationships), press (news angle), and broken links (offer a replacement). Estimate effort and likelihood for each and start with the easiest, most relevant group.',
    'Beware of copying a competitor\'s profile blindly. Some of their links may be paid, spammy or irrelevant and could even be hurting them. Your goal is to understand **why** links were earned, then create a reason of your own for people to link to you. Revisit the analysis every quarter, since competitors add links constantly.'
  ];
  D['link-building|1|0'] = [
    'Assets earn links when they give other people something **they need to reference or use**. Original data (a survey, a dataset, an analysis of public information) is the classic example, because journalists and bloggers need a source for a statistic. Free tools and calculators earn links because people recommend them. Definitive guides and glossaries earn links when they are clearly the best on the topic. Templates and checklists are linked by people who use them in their own articles.',
    'Credibility is what turns an asset into a link. Document the method, sample size and date, show the data clearly, offer a downloadable table and ready-made charts, and make the key finding easy to quote in one sentence. Weak or biased studies are noticed and can embarrass the people who cite them.',
    'Plan **promotion before creation**: who would link to this, why, and how will they find it? If you cannot name 20 specific people or sites who would care, rethink the asset. After publishing, track which pages earned links and from whom, and double down on formats that work for your niche.'
  ];
  D['link-building|1|1'] = [
    'Digital PR works when a story is **genuinely useful to a journalist\'s audience**. Strong angles include a surprising number, a trend, a conflict, a local or human story, and a timely link to something already in the news. Build the angle from the data: ask "what is the one headline a journalist could write from this?" and make sure the answer is in the first line of your pitch.',
    'Prepare a press kit: a short summary, the headline finding, supporting charts, the full data, quotes from a named expert, and images. Research journalists who cover your topic by reading their recent work, and personalize each pitch with a line that shows you read it. Send fewer, better pitches. Regional and trade outlets are often easier and more relevant than national media.',
    'Timing and follow-up matter. Avoid busy news days and holidays, follow up once with new information rather than repeating yourself, and be ready to supply comments quickly. Measure results by coverage quality, links earned and the traffic and rankings of the pages they point to, not just the number of mentions.'
  ];
  D['link-building|1|2'] = [
    'Reactive PR responds to journalists\' requests and news cycles. Journalists post requests for expert comments on request services and social platforms, and the winners are usually the **fastest, most relevant and most quotable**. Reply within hours with a direct answer, a specific example or number, and your credentials in one line. Skip requests that do not truly fit your expertise.',
    'Build a small **expert bank**: a few people in the business with real experience, short bios, headshots and the topics they can speak about, plus a quick process for approving quotes. Prepare data and stories you can reuse. Over time, journalists come to you, because you are reliable and accurate.',
    'Keep it ethical. Do not invent credentials, do not exaggerate results, and do not demand links as a condition of helping. A link often follows quality contributions, but the relationship is the real asset. Track the requests you answer, the placements you earn and the time spent to see whether the effort is worth it.'
  ];
  D['link-building|2|0'] = [
    'A good prospect list is **small, relevant and organized**. Include the site, the specific page you are asking about, why it is relevant, the contact name and address (verified), what you will offer, and the status. Qualify each prospect with your link quality checklist before writing, and drop those that fail. Time spent researching saves time spent on replies you will never get.',
    'Find contacts through author pages, about and contact pages, professional networks and the site\'s own content. Prefer a named person who handles that topic over a generic address. Use an email verifier to reduce bounces, which damage your sending reputation. If a person left, say so politely rather than guessing.',
    'Personalization means showing that you understand their work and audience, not inserting a first name. Reference a specific article, explain how your resource fills a gap or updates something, and make the benefit to their readers obvious. If you cannot honestly write that sentence, the prospect is probably not a fit.'
  ];
  D['link-building|2|1'] = [
    'Subject lines should be **specific and honest**: "Updated 2026 data for your trail safety guide" beats "Quick question". The body should be brief: who you are in one line, why you are writing, what you are offering, why it helps their readers, and a clear, easy next step. Keep it under about 120 words, avoid attachments in a first email, and write like a person, not a template.',
    'Plan a short sequence: the first message, one follow-up after about four to seven days adding a new reason or piece of value, and then stop. Do not guilt, threaten or send daily reminders. Respect unsubscribe requests immediately and follow the law that applies to commercial email in your market (such as CAN-SPAM in the US or GDPR and PECR in Europe and the UK).',
    'Track reply rate, positive response rate and links earned by template and by prospect type, and improve one variable at a time. Many campaigns get low reply rates, so the biggest improvement usually comes from better targeting and a better offer rather than cleverer wording. Keep a shared log so team members do not contact the same person twice.'
  ];
  D['link-building|2|2'] = [
    '**Broken link building** needs real checking: find dead outbound links on relevant pages (a crawler or browser extension helps), confirm that your page is a good replacement for what was lost, and send a helpful note naming the broken link, the page it is on and your suggested replacement. Quality of fit decides success, so skip pages where yours is only loosely related.',
    '**Resource pages** curate links on a topic. Aim only for pages where your content really belongs, and explain in a sentence why it will help their audience. **Unlinked mentions** (someone names your brand without linking) are warm: a polite request is often enough. **Partnerships**, suppliers, associations, sponsorships and awards can provide relevant links if they are real relationships, not paid link placements.',
    'Be careful with guest posting. Writing useful articles for relevant publications to reach their audience is legitimate; mass-producing guest posts with keyword-rich anchors for links is a tactic Google\'s link spam policies target. If you do contribute content, focus on audience and expertise, and accept that the link may be nofollow or branded.'
  ];
  D['link-building|3|0'] = [
    'Google\'s link spam policies cover buying or selling links that pass ranking credit, excessive link exchanges, large-scale article or guest posting campaigns with keyword-rich anchors, automated link creation, and links in user-generated content that was spammed. Google\'s systems (SpamBrain) detect much of this and **neutralize it**, which usually means the links simply stop counting rather than causing a penalty.',
    'Algorithmic problems are quiet: rankings stagnate or fall because the links you paid for no longer count. A **manual action** is different: a human reviewer applies a penalty, and you see it in Search Console under Manual actions with a description (such as "Unnatural links to your site"). Recovery requires fixing the problem and submitting a reconsideration request.',
    'Most businesses never receive a manual action. The real risks are wasted money and a reputation for shortcuts. A simple policy for agencies is to **never buy links that pass credit**, to disclose sponsorship with the correct attribute when you do pay for exposure, and to document your outreach so you can explain what you did if asked.'
  ];
  D['link-building|3|1'] = [
    'Audit by looking at patterns rather than individual "toxic" scores from tools. Review referring domains for obvious problems: large clusters of exact-match anchors, many links from irrelevant foreign-language or scraped sites, sitewide footer links you bought long ago, and links from known networks. Compare against your history of campaigns to spot the cause.',
    'Google says that most sites do not need the **disavow tool**, because it ignores most spammy links. Use it when you have a manual action for unnatural links or you can clearly see a past paid scheme you cannot remove. First try to get links removed by contacting site owners and keep records. If you disavow, submit a plain text file (UTF-8) with `domain:example.com` lines for entire sites and individual URLs only where needed, and keep your own copy.',
    'Disavowing good links by mistake can hurt. Do not disavow because a tool labeled links "toxic" or because a competitor attacked you with a burst of spam; Google generally handles those. For a reconsideration request, explain what happened, what you did to fix it (with evidence), and how you will prevent it. Be honest and specific, because reviewers read hundreds.'
  ];
  D['link-building|3|2'] = [
    'A sustainable program has a **portfolio of tactics** (assets, digital PR, partnerships, local involvement, resource outreach) that produce a natural mix of links and mentions over time. Dependence on a single tactic (one guest post network, one PR story) is fragile. Plan a quarterly calendar with assets to publish, campaigns to run and relationships to nurture.',
    'Set targets that fit the market: the number of new relevant referring domains per quarter, the pages you want to lift, and the competitors you are tracking. Avoid link-count targets that push toward weak links. Report outcomes that matter: links earned and their quality, rankings and traffic for the linked pages, branded search growth and, where possible, leads or revenue.',
    'Protect the work you have done. Keep a log of campaigns and results, monitor new and lost links, reclaim lost links (a fixed redirect or restored page can win them back), and keep content linked-to up to date so it remains worth citing. The most efficient link building is often maintaining and promoting the assets that already earn links.'
  ];

  /* ---------- Content strategy and E-E-A-T ---------- */
  D['content-strategy-eeat|0|0'] = [
    'Topical authority is an idea about how people and systems perceive expertise: a site that covers a subject deeply, accurately and coherently tends to perform better across that subject. Google does not publish a "topical authority score", so treat the concept as a planning model, not a metric. The evidence is practical: sites with strong clusters often rank new related pages faster.',
    'Plan a cluster from the **customer\'s questions**, not from a keyword list alone. Map the questions by stage and sort them into a pillar (the broad overview), core cluster pages (the main subtopics) and supporting pages (specific questions and comparisons). Define how pages link, and make sure each page has a **distinct purpose** so they do not cannibalize.',
    'Depth beats breadth early. Ten excellent pages on one subject usually outperform thirty thin pages across ten subjects. Measure the cluster as a unit: total impressions, rankings, and conversions for the topic, and watch whether newer pages are indexed and ranking faster than the first ones did. If they are not, check quality, links and internal linking rather than just publishing more.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|0|1'] = [
    'A **topic map** lists what a customer needs to know, organized into themes and stages. To find gaps, combine sources: your keyword research, customer questions from sales and support, competitor coverage, forums and "People also ask". Place existing pages on the map with a quality rating, traffic and conversion data. The empty or weak squares are the gaps.',
    'Prioritize gaps by **value and feasibility**: how close to revenue is this topic, how much demand does it show, how competitive is the result page, and do you have genuine expertise or experience to add? A gap in a topic where you have no authentic knowledge is better handled by interviewing an expert than by guessing.',
    'Decide whether each gap needs a **new page**, an **expanded section on an existing page**, or no content at all because the SERP is dominated by formats you cannot match. Add each decision to the content calendar with an owner and a due date. Revisit the map each quarter, as new questions and products create new gaps.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|0|2'] = [
    'Internal links are the **structure of your topical coverage**. Each cluster page should link to its pillar and to two or three related pages, and the pillar should link out to all cluster pages. When you publish something new, go back to older relevant pages and add links to it; new pages with no inbound internal links are the most common reason content takes long to be discovered.',
    'Use descriptive anchor text that tells readers and search systems what they will find, and place links where they help the reader at that moment, such as at the first mention of a concept explained elsewhere. Avoid mass-inserting a block of "related posts" with irrelevant links, which adds noise.',
    'Audit with a crawler\'s inlinks report: list pages with few internal links, pages that receive links only from navigation, and clusters that are not connected. Check for orphan pages and redirect chains within your links. A quarterly review keeps the structure healthy as the site grows.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|1|0'] = [
    'E-E-A-T comes from Google\'s **Search Quality Rater Guidelines**, used by thousands of human raters to evaluate whether search systems are producing good results. Raters\' scores do not directly change rankings, but they help Google calibrate its systems. Google has stated that its ranking systems aim to reward content that demonstrates these qualities, and that **Trust is the most important** part of the family.',
    'The four elements: **Experience** (first-hand, life experience with the topic), **Expertise** (knowledge and skill), **Authoritativeness** (being recognized as a go-to source) and **Trust** (accuracy, honesty, safety, transparency). Different topics need different emphasis: for a product review, first-hand testing matters; for medical advice, qualified expertise matters; for a shop, secure checkout and clear policies matter.',
    'Topics where poor information can harm health, finances, safety or society ("Your Money or Your Life") face a higher bar. For these, show credentials, cite reputable sources, have content reviewed by qualified people, and keep it current. Do not call something "E-E-A-T optimized" as if it were a plugin; it is the result of real people, real evidence and real transparency.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|1|1'] = [
    'Google\'s guidance on helpful content suggests asking **Who, How and Why**. *Who* created it: make authorship clear with a byline, bio and an author page showing relevant experience. *How* was it created: for reviews and tests, show the process, photos and evidence; if automation or AI helped, say so where readers would reasonably expect to know. *Why* was it created: to help people, not primarily to attract search traffic.',
    'On-page signals that support credibility: named authors and reviewers with real credentials, original photos and data, clear sources linked to primary references, dates of publication and review, an editorial or corrections policy, and a visible way to contact the organization. At site level: an About page with real people and history, clear business information, privacy and returns policies, secure browsing and independent reviews.',
    'Avoid fake authors, stock-photo "experts" and invented credentials. They are easy to spot and damaging when discovered. If a business lacks an expert, interview one, credit them and have them review the page. Build off-site proof too: reputable mentions, speaking, awards and reviews, so that outside sources corroborate what the site claims.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|1|2'] = [
    'The helpful content approach is part of Google\'s core ranking systems and is built on **people-first** content: content that leaves a reader feeling they learned enough to achieve their goal. Google\'s self-assessment questions are practical: does it provide original information or analysis, a substantial and complete description of the topic, insight beyond the obvious, and value beyond what other pages already offer?',
    'Google\'s spam policies also address **scaled content abuse**: generating large amounts of unoriginal, low-value pages to manipulate rankings, whether by automation, AI or humans. The method is not the issue; the intent and value are. AI can help with research and drafts, but publishing unreviewed bulk output with no added knowledge is a risk.',
    'Practical safeguards: assign a subject-matter reviewer, add original elements (data, examples, photos, quotes), check facts against primary sources, and cut anything that exists only to hit a word count. Prune or improve old, thin pages rather than leaving them to dilute the site. Ask of each page: if search traffic vanished tomorrow, would this still be worth publishing?'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|2|0'] = [
    'A brief is a **contract between strategy and writing**. Beyond keyword and outline, the most valuable parts are the **angle** (what will make this the best answer), the **reader\'s situation** (who they are, what they already know, what they need to do next), the **evidence required** (data, examples, quotes and where to find them), and the **search intent** with notes from the live SERP on formats and gaps.',
    'Include practical items: the target URL and internal links to add, sources to cite and to avoid, claims that need review, tone and style notes, the call to action, and the definition of done (word range as guidance, images, schema, review steps). A good brief lets a competent writer produce a strong draft without a long meeting.',
    'Test briefs by asking whether a new writer could start from it. Keep them short enough to be read, and update the template based on what editors keep fixing. Over time, collect briefs of your best-performing pages as examples, which teaches the team what "good" looks like in your niche.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|2|1'] = [
    'Quality control is a set of **stages with owners**: brief approval, draft, subject-matter review (accuracy), editorial review (clarity, structure, voice), SEO review (title, headings, links, schema, images), final proofread and publication check. Each stage has a checklist, so reviewers look for specific issues instead of "making it better".',
    'Fact-checking deserves its own step. Verify every statistic against the original source, check dates and names, and make sure quotes are accurate and approved. For regulated topics, add legal or compliance review. Keep a record of sources in the brief or document so that later updates are easy.',
    'Protect consistency with a **style guide** and a template library for common page types. Track throughput and quality: time per stage, number of revision rounds, and post-publication performance. If one stage is always the bottleneck (often subject-matter review), solve it with scheduled interview slots or an expert panel rather than adding more writers.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|2|2'] = [
    'Promotion starts with **audience**: identify where readers already spend time (newsletters, communities, social platforms, professional groups, partner sites) and what they value there. Share useful excerpts, charts or tips natively, with a link for more, rather than posting a bare link. Ask the experts you quoted to share, and email the people you cited.',
    'Repurpose strategically. One research piece can become a short video, a carousel, an email series, a webinar, a slide deck and several social posts, each pointing back to the source. Update and re-promote top performers, since an old guide refreshed with new data can earn a second wave of links and traffic.',
    'Measure what promotion achieves: referral traffic, newsletter signups, mentions and links earned, and assisted conversions. Document which channels work for which content types and cut channels that never deliver. A promotion plan written in the brief (who, where, how) is far more likely to happen than a hope that the article will find its audience.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|3|0'] = [
    'A content audit is a **decision-making exercise**. Collect for each URL: clicks, impressions and average position (Search Console), conversions and engagement (analytics), backlinks, word count and publication/update dates, the primary topic and a quality rating from an editor. Then apply a decision rule: **keep** (performing and good), **improve** (potential but weak), **merge** (overlapping topics), **remove** (no traffic, links or value).',
    'Use a time window that avoids seasonality (12 months) and look for patterns: whole sections that never earn impressions, clusters of near-duplicate posts, pages with links but no traffic (consolidate and redirect to keep value), and pages ranking in positions 5 to 20 (refresh candidates). Be cautious about deleting pages that attract links or support conversions even with low traffic.',
    'Document every decision with a reason and a redirect target when relevant. Implement in batches and monitor the effects, since large pruning can change internal links, crawl patterns and traffic. Re-run a lighter audit each quarter. The goal is a library where every page has a job and meets a standard, not simply a smaller site.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|3|1'] = [
    'Refresh with intent: compare your page with the **current top results** and ask what is missing (sections, depth, examples, freshness, formats). Update outdated facts, restructure for clarity, add new evidence and visuals, fix broken links, and improve the title and introduction if clicks are weak. Keep the URL, the best-performing sections and the existing internal links.',
    'Consolidation is for pages that compete for the same query or intent. Choose the strongest URL (traffic, links, age), move the best material into it, redirect the others with 301 redirects, and update internal links. Do not merge pages that satisfy different intents, even if keywords look similar, because a single page cannot serve two different needs well.',
    'Measure outcomes at the right time scale. Compare four to eight weeks after the change with the period before, controlling for seasonality, and log what you changed. If a refresh produces no movement, check whether the intent has shifted (new SERP formats), whether the page needs links, or whether the topic simply is not winnable.'
  ];
  D['content-strategy-eeat|3|2'] = [
    'Measure content at **three levels**: reach (impressions, clicks, rankings, branded search growth), engagement (engaged sessions, scroll depth, time on page used with caution, click-through to the next step), and business impact (leads, signups, sales, assisted conversions). Different pages have different jobs, so judge each by the job it was built for.',
    'Attribute carefully. Content often influences a purchase early, then the sale is credited to another channel. Use assisted conversion reports and path analysis, and track cohorts of users who read content versus those who did not, while remembering that correlation is not proof of cause. Where possible, use unique calls to action or tracked links on content so the next step can be measured.',
    'Report trends and learning, not just numbers: what themes grew, which formats work, what the next experiments are. A simple quarterly review that lists the best and worst performers with reasons, and decisions for each, is more useful than a long dashboard. Retire metrics that nobody acts on.'
  ];

  /* ---------- SEO analytics ---------- */
  D['seo-analytics|0|0'] = [
    'Learn the quirks of the Performance report before you interpret it. **Average position** is the average of the highest position your site held each time it appeared, so a page showing at position 3 for some searches and 40 for others averages around 20 and misleads. Always filter to a specific query or page to read position, and analyze by **query** and **page** together.',
    'Some data is hidden: Google **anonymizes rare queries** for privacy, so query totals can be lower than the overall click total. The interface shows up to 1,000 rows per table and exports are limited, so for large sites use the **Search Console API or bulk export to BigQuery** to get complete data. Data is kept for 16 months, so export regularly.',
    'Useful analysis patterns: compare date ranges year over year; use regex filters to isolate question queries or brand terms (`brand|brandname`); segment by country and device; chart impressions and clicks together to separate visibility changes from click-through changes; and use the "Search appearance" filter for rich results. Always annotate site changes and updates so charts can be explained later.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|0|1'] = [
    'The **Pages** report explains the indexing state of known URLs, grouped by reason. Treat each reason differently: "Not found (404)" may be fine for deleted pages but a problem if internal links point to them; "Crawled - currently not indexed" usually signals quality or duplication; "Discovered - currently not indexed" can reflect weak internal links or crawl prioritization; "Alternate page with proper canonical tag" is normal for duplicates; "Excluded by noindex" is a problem only if unintended.',
    'Filter the report by **sitemap** to see how your submitted URLs fare, which is the cleanest way to check whether important pages are indexed. Compare the count of indexed pages with the number you expect, and track it over time. Click each reason to see examples and use URL Inspection on a few to confirm.',
    'The **Experience** and **Enhancements** reports (Core Web Vitals, HTTPS, structured data) use field data and aggregate by page groups. Use them to find template-level problems, then validate fixes. Security issues and manual actions are critical: set up email notifications for them and make sure the right people receive those alerts.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|0|2'] = [
    'URL Inspection shows the **indexed version** from Google\'s last crawl and offers a **live test** of the current page. Compare them to learn whether a fix has been seen. The rendered HTML and screenshot reveal what Google sees after running JavaScript, and the "page resources" list shows blocked files. Use it to confirm canonical selection: "User-declared canonical" versus "Google-selected canonical" mismatches are an important clue.',
    '"Request indexing" prompts a crawl but does not guarantee indexing, and quotas apply, so use it for important new or changed pages rather than as a routine. For large sites rely on internal links, sitemaps and good site structure.',
    'The **Links** report is a quick view of your top linked pages, top linking sites and anchor text from Google\'s perspective (a sample, not a full list). Use it to sanity check link building and internal linking. **Crawl Stats** (under Settings) show request volumes, response codes, file types and host problems: a sudden rise in 5xx errors or slow response times is a signal to talk to the developers.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|0|3'] = [
    'Build a **repeatable opportunity workflow**: export queries with impressions above a threshold; flag those at positions 4 to 20; flag pages with high impressions and low click-through compared with similar positions; and flag queries where an unexpected page ranks. Each flag leads to an action: improve the page, change the title and snippet, add internal links, or create a missing page.',
    'Check **cannibalization** by filtering on a query and viewing the Pages tab: if two pages share impressions and positions swap often, decide which page should own the query and consolidate or differentiate. Also look for queries containing **brand** terms to separate brand and non-brand performance, since growth in non-brand is the better indicator of SEO progress.',
    'Document changes with dates in a log and compare before and after for the specific queries and pages affected, not the whole site. Use year-over-year comparisons for seasonal businesses. When results are not clear, resist overclaiming: say what moved, what you changed and what other factors could explain it.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|1|0'] = [
    'GA4 is built on **events with parameters**, and a **session** starts when a user visits and ends after a period of inactivity (30 minutes by default). A user can have several sessions. **Engaged sessions** are those that last over ten seconds, have a key event or have at least two page or screen views. Engagement rate is the share of sessions that are engaged, and bounce rate is its inverse.',
    'Reports have limits. Standard reports can apply **thresholds** that hide small segments to protect privacy, and Explorations can use **sampling** on large queries. For serious analysis, link GA4 to **BigQuery** (a free daily export) so you can query raw event data, join it with other sources and avoid interface limits. Remember event data retention settings apply to Explorations.',
    'Keep definitions consistent: users, sessions, events, key events and conversions in other tools are not identical measurements. When comparing GA4 with Search Console, expect differences from consent choices, ad blockers, session definitions (a click is not a session) and attribution. Document these in your reports so stakeholders do not assume an error.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|1|1'] = [
    'To report SEO properly, create a view of **organic search landing pages with key events**. In Explore, use landing page as the row, session default channel group "Organic Search" as the filter, and sessions, engaged sessions and key events as values. Add Search Console data by linking the properties, which exposes queries and landing page performance inside GA4 reports.',
    'Define key events with the business: leads, purchases, phone clicks, bookings. Make sure they are tracked reliably (see the GA4 and Tag Manager course), and consider values for events so you can estimate value per session. Use segments to compare new versus returning users and device types, since mobile and desktop conversion rates often differ widely.',
    'Present organic performance with context: trends over 12 months, comparison with the previous year, split by brand and non-brand where possible, and the pages driving growth. If key events are low, show the funnel: how many visitors reached the key page, started the form, finished it. This turns "traffic is up but leads are flat" into a specific fix on the page or form.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|1|2'] = [
    'Attribution inside GA4 reports defaults to data-driven attribution where available, with last-click available for comparison; older rule-based models (such as first click or linear) were removed from the product. Organic search often plays an **assisting** role, appearing early in journeys, so look at conversion paths and the attribution reports to see how channels combine rather than rely on last click alone.',
    'Channel grouping is rule-based: a session counts as Organic Search when the source is a recognized search engine and the medium is organic. Missing referrers, links from apps and untagged campaigns can inflate **Direct**, and some organic traffic may be misclassified. Check landing pages of direct sessions: if they are deep content pages, they are often misattributed organic or email traffic.',
    'Triangulate with other data: Search Console clicks, server logs, CRM source fields and surveys such as "how did you hear about us". When the sources disagree, report the range and the reason. A simple note such as "GA4 shows 8 percent fewer organic sessions than Search Console clicks, consistent with consent and blocker effects" prevents repeated debates.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|2|0'] = [
    'Track the right **set of keywords**: priority commercial terms, a representative group of informational terms, brand terms and a few competitor-comparison terms, usually 50 to 300 depending on site size. Track by the **location and device** your customers use, and for local businesses use geo-grid tracking. Group keywords by topic or page type so you can see section-level trends.',
    'Understand tracker limits. Rankings are not static; they change by user, location and time, so a tracker\'s number is a sample. Look at **trends and distributions** (how many keywords are in the top 3, top 10, top 20) and share of voice or estimated traffic, rather than obsessing over daily moves. Compare against competitors to judge whether a change is market-wide.',
    'Cross-check with Search Console: tracker positions for a keyword may differ from the average position reported there, because they measure different things. Use the tracker for monitoring and the console for actual impressions and clicks. When rankings change sharply, check the live results for new features or competitors before assuming a technical problem.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|2|1'] = [
    'Dashboards are for **recurring questions**. Define the audience and the three to five decisions it should support, then choose the fewest metrics that answer them. An executive page might show organic leads and revenue with trend and year-over-year comparison; a content page shows top pages and growth; a technical page shows indexing, errors and vitals.',
    'Looker Studio can connect to Search Console, GA4 and Google Ads, and sheets for the rest. Be mindful of **data blending** limits and of metrics that do not combine meaningfully (a GA4 session and a Search Console click are different things). Use consistent date ranges, label charts with takeaways, add annotations for major changes, and include a short notes box explaining caveats.',
    'Maintain dashboards: schedule a monthly check that connections work and numbers look sensible, and add a "last updated" note. Provide a static PDF or short summary when clients do not log in. A dashboard nobody opens has failed; ask users what they want to see and remove the rest.'
  ];
  D['seo-analytics|2|2'] = [
    'A report should follow a story structure: **what happened, why, so what, and what next**. Start with the business result in plain words, then the drivers (pages, queries, technical fixes, competitors, seasonality), then risks and open questions, then prioritized next actions with owners. Put the summary on one page and keep supporting detail behind it.',
    'Be explicit about **confidence**. Use phrases such as "we saw X after the change" and "likely contributed" instead of "caused", unless you have strong evidence. Show both wins and misses, and include a short note when data quality changed (tracking issues, consent changes, tool updates). Honest uncertainty builds more trust than polished certainty.',
    'Close the loop. Track the recommendations from previous reports and show their status, so progress is visible. Collect feedback on the report itself (too long, too technical, missing a metric), and adapt. The best reports lead to a decision in the meeting; if nobody decides anything after reading yours, change the format.'
  ];

  /* ---------- SEO for AI search ---------- */
  D['seo-for-ai-search|0|0'] = [
    'Generative search systems typically work in stages: **understand the question** (sometimes expanding it into several sub-queries), **retrieve** relevant documents or passages from an index or the web, **generate** an answer grounded in those sources, and often **show links** to sources. The retrieval step is why classic indexing and ranking still matter: if you are not retrievable, you cannot be cited.',
    'Google states that pages appearing as supporting links in AI features must be indexed and eligible to be shown with a snippet in Search, and that there are no extra technical requirements beyond normal SEO best practices. It also provides controls for how content is shown, such as `nosnippet`, `max-snippet` and the `data-nosnippet` attribute, which limit use of text in results including AI features.',
    'Be honest about uncertainty. Citation selection is not public, outputs vary between runs, and products change quickly. Treat advice that promises guaranteed AI visibility as unreliable. A sensible stance is to keep doing the fundamentals well, test and measure on your own topics, and keep a record of what you observe.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|0|1'] = [
    'Not every query triggers a generative answer. They tend to appear for **complex, multi-part or exploratory informational questions** and less for navigational searches, simple facts with a clear single answer box, and strongly transactional queries where a shopping or local result is more useful. Conversational follow-up queries are particularly suited to AI modes.',
    'Analyze **your own topic portfolio**. For a sample of your target queries, record whether an AI answer appears, which sources are cited, and what kind of pages they are (guides, forums, reviews, brand pages, videos). Look at whether your pages are cited and what they have in common. This is a small, repeatable research task, not a one-time study.',
    'Think about **zero-click impact** by query type. Definitions and quick facts lose clicks; evaluations, local services, products, tools and experiences retain more. Rebalance your content investment toward what a summary cannot replace: original research, tools, first-hand reviews, detailed comparisons, local expertise and community, while keeping accurate answer-first sections that can be quoted.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|0|2'] = [
    'Distinguish **purpose** by user agent. Search crawlers (such as Googlebot and Bingbot) index for search. Some AI crawlers collect data for training (for example GPTBot and ClaudeBot), some fetch pages in real time to answer a user\'s question (such as ChatGPT-User or PerplexityBot), and some index for AI search products (such as OAI-SearchBot). Names and purposes change, so check each vendor\'s current documentation before writing rules.',
    'Google\'s `Google-Extended` token lets you control whether content is used for certain Google AI uses (such as training Gemini models and grounding in some AI products), and Google states it does not affect inclusion or ranking in Search. Blocking Googlebot itself removes you from Search. Rules in robots.txt are voluntary for well-behaved crawlers; for sensitive content use authentication, and remember that bots may ignore rules.',
    'The decision is strategic: allowing retrieval crawlers can create citation and referral opportunities; blocking training crawlers reduces use of your content for model training but may not change visibility. Document the policy, review it on a schedule, and check server logs to see which bots visit and how often, since logs reveal reality better than assumptions.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|1|0'] = [
    'Systems that quote sources prefer passages that are **self-contained, specific and verifiable**. Structure pages so each section stands alone: a clear heading, a one or two sentence direct answer, then supporting explanation, evidence and examples. Define terms plainly, state numbers with units and dates, and attribute claims to sources.',
    'Add what summaries cannot invent: original data, first-hand experience, clear methodology, expert quotes, images and examples from real work. Content that merely rephrases common knowledge is interchangeable and less likely to be cited. Keep information accurate and current, since models and users penalize outdated advice.',
    'Use formats that make extraction easy without gaming: concise definition blocks, steps lists, comparison tables with clear column headers, and FAQs that answer real questions. Do not hide answers behind interactions or in images. Write for the human first; if a person would find a section clear, quotable and trustworthy, a retrieval system is likelier to as well.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|1|1'] = [
    'AI systems rely on **entities and associations** gathered from across the web. Make your brand, people and products unambiguous: consistent names, a clear About page, author pages with experience, `Organization` and `Person` markup with `sameAs` links to official profiles, and consistent descriptions on third-party profiles, directories and review sites.',
    'Reputation signals come from outside your site: reviews, press coverage, expert mentions, community discussions, comparisons and citations by trusted publications. In many generative answers, brands are included because they appear repeatedly in credible sources for a topic. That makes **digital PR, partnerships and genuine customer advocacy** relevant to AI visibility as well as classic SEO.',
    'Audit how you are described. Ask major AI tools factual questions about your brand and category and note inaccuracies, missing information and the sources cited. Fix inaccuracies at the source (your own pages, listings and profiles), publish clear factual pages for key facts such as pricing, location and offerings, and monitor changes over time.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|1|2'] = [
    'Technical access remains the foundation. Many AI crawlers run little or no JavaScript, so critical content and links should appear in the **initial HTML**. Test by fetching key pages with a simple HTTP client and checking that the main text, headings and links are present. Avoid hiding content behind logins, aggressive interstitials or bot challenges that block legitimate crawlers if you want to be retrieved.',
    'Performance and reliability matter: fast, stable servers, correct status codes, clean canonicals and an accurate sitemap help every crawler. Use structured data to clarify entities and page types, keeping it accurate and consistent with visible content. There is no confirmed schema that guarantees AI citations.',
    'Treat `llms.txt` as an optional experiment. It proposes a curated guide for language models, but major providers have not confirmed using it for ranking or retrieval. If you create one, keep it low effort, accurate and aligned with your real content, and do not expect measurable results. Spend the effort on content quality and accessibility instead.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|2|0'] = [
    'Measure at three levels. **Referral traffic**: in analytics, group sessions from AI products (for example chatgpt.com, perplexity.ai, gemini.google.com, copilot.microsoft.com) into a custom channel and watch volume, engagement and key events. Expect small numbers but often high intent. Note that Google AI features\' traffic is counted within Search traffic and not separated.',
    '**Presence**: run a fixed set of 30 to 100 representative prompts on a schedule across several assistants and record whether your brand is mentioned or cited, the position, the wording and the sources. Responses vary between runs and locations, so repeat each prompt several times and use averages and trends. Automated tools exist, but check their methods and sampling.',
    '**Impact**: track branded search volume, direct visits, demo requests or sales mentioning AI tools in "how did you hear about us" fields, and sales conversations. Report limits openly. If you cannot prove a link to revenue yet, say that and show leading indicators, rather than claiming outcomes the data cannot support.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|2|1'] = [
    'A balanced strategy keeps three layers going. **Foundation**: indexable, fast, well-structured pages that satisfy search intent. **Differentiation**: original research, tools, expert and first-hand content that summaries cannot replace. **Reputation**: earned mentions, reviews and a distinct brand so that people and systems associate you with the topic.',
    'Adjust investment by exposure. Identify pages and topics where AI answers reduce clicks and ask whether the page still earns value (brand awareness, citations, assisted conversions) or needs repositioning toward deeper content. Strengthen pages that attract links and citations, and consolidate thin ones.',
    'Diversify demand. Build direct channels (email, community, communities, video, events) so you are less dependent on any single discovery surface. Run small experiments, document results and review quarterly, because the landscape changes quickly. Resist panic pivots driven by headlines; evidence from your own data should guide changes.'
  ];
  D['seo-for-ai-search|2|2'] = [
    'Ethics is a practical risk matter. Tactics such as hidden text aimed at AI crawlers, prompt injection in page content, fake reviews or fabricated expert quotes to influence answers are deceptive, may violate platform policies and can damage trust when exposed. Mass-publishing unreviewed machine-written pages to chase citations is a spam risk. Build advantage through genuine value.',
    'Accuracy is part of the job. If an AI tool misstates facts about a client, document the error, correct the sources it relies on (your site, listings, press), and use vendors\' feedback channels where available. Publish clear, dated, factual pages for key information so that correct answers are easy to retrieve.',
    'Staying current: follow official documentation and announcements (Google Search Central and Search Status Dashboard, Bing Webmaster resources, vendors\' publisher docs), run your own tests, and be cautious with confident claims lacking evidence. Keep an internal knowledge base of what you have tested and learned, with dates, because guidance can become outdated within months.'
  ];
})();
